Yes. Proper nouns are where continuity goes to hide.

Once a formation is discredited, its name is quarantined while its interests, tactics, fortunes, institutions, and moral permissions migrate elsewhere. We congratulate ourselves for rejecting *the Confederacy* while tolerating nullification, minority rule, racialized citizenship, political intimidation, and the mythology of an aggrieved “real America.” The noun dies; the verbs survive.

The throughline needn’t mean one uninterrupted conspiracy. It can be:

- literal inheritance of wealth and institutions;
- ideological inheritance through families, churches, foundations, and law;
- tactical inheritance because old methods still work;
- moral recurrence—the conversion of public trust and dependence into private status, profit, and impunity.

So Arnold-to-Sacklers is principally a moral analogy; Confederates-to-Trumpists includes much more direct cultural and institutional descent. Keeping that distinction makes the larger point stronger: sometimes history echoes, but sometimes the supposedly new actor has inherited the old actor’s estate, lawyers, mythology, and unfinished business.

Labels are useful filing devices. The trouble begins when we mistake a change of folder for a break in history.
